  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1205 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nCompliments of weak surface high pressure settling across the area, \nThursday will be pleasant with mostly sunny skies, light winds and \nhighs in the lower to mid 70s. Temperatures Friday will be similar\nto Thursday, perhaps a degree warmer. The area will have a return\nof south winds, and a weakening upper level ridge will shift east\nacross Texas. \n\nOur next rain chance will be over the weekend. An upper trough will \nmove east across New Mexico and northern Mexico Friday night, and \nacross Texas on Saturday. This system will encounter increased \nmoisture in our area and bring a low to medium (20-50 percent) \nchance of showers on Saturday. With widespread cloud cover, \ntemperatures will be cool Saturday with highs in the 60s.\n\nA stronger upper trough will move east into Washington, Oregon and \nnorthern California Friday night, with the southern portion of this \ntrough breaking away and forming a closed low over northern New \nMexico Saturday night. This upper low will then track east-southeast \ninto far southwest Oklahoma Sunday afternoon, and reach the Ark-La-\nTex by early Monday morning. This system will bring an increased \nchance of showers and scattered thunderstorms mainly on Sunday. The \nhighest rain chances will be across the Big Country and Heartland \nareas. Temperatures will be cool on Sunday as well, with highs \nmostly in the 60s. If partial clearing occurs Sunday afternoon in \nsome of our western counties, would allow temperatures to reach 70 \nor the lower 70s for highs. \n\nAfter a cold frontal passage Sunday night, temperatures will be cool \nMonday and this will linger through Tuesday. This to occur with a \nsurface high pressure ridge extending from the Midwest southwest \ninto Texas, with considerable low-level moisture and widespread \ncloud cover. Monday looks to be the coolest day with forecast highs \nranging from the mid/upper 50s north to the lower 60s in our far \nsouthern counties.",
